auth {
  mode: oauth2
}

docs {
  Three ways to drive auth from here — all of them the real protocol; there is deliberately no
  password grant to shortcut any of it:

  1. "Auth / Sign In (Scripted)" — the fast front door. Its pre-request script drives the whole
     authorization code + PKCE dance (sign-in form, {{signin_email}} / {{signin_password}},
     authorize, exchange) with no browser window and no cached sessions — every run is a fresh
     sign-in, so switching users is just editing the env vars. Exports {{access_token}} and
     {{refresh_token}}; "Refresh Token (Scripted)" rolls them forward.

  2. "Auth / Sign In (Browser)" — the same dance through a real browser window, via the
     collection's OAuth2 settings below. Slower, but it exercises the sign-in page the way a
     person sees it. Also exports {{access_token}}.

  3. The raw pairs in "Auth / Account" — the account flows one HTTP request at a time, for
     seeing the anti-enumeration answers and token handling on the wire.

  Requests that need a user token carry `Bearer {{access_token}}` — whichever sign-in ran last
  wins.

  Prerequisites: docker compose up -d, auth on :5100, and the worker on :5200 (it delivers the
  emails to Mailpit at http://localhost:8025).
}

auth:oauth2 {
  grant_type: authorization_code
  callback_url: {{callback_url}}
  authorization_url: {{auth_url}}/connect/authorize
  access_token_url: {{auth_url}}/connect/token
  refresh_token_url: {{auth_url}}/connect/token
  client_id: {{client_id}}
  client_secret:
  scope: openid email profile roles api.read api.write offline_access
  state:
  pkce: true
  credentials_placement: body
  token_placement: header
  token_header_prefix: Bearer
  auto_fetch_token: true
  auto_refresh_token: true
}
